
======================================================================================================================
  K  THE ONE IDENTITY, AND WHY ROUTE A IS THE KERNEL ON WHICH THIS CLASS OF TEST IS SHARPEST
======================================================================================================================
  Everything in this file reduces to two logarithmic slopes of the SAME kernel:
      L_mu(x) = dln mu / dln x   (>= 0, mu increasing)          the AQUAL / MG slope
      L_nu(y) = dln nu / dln y   (<= 0, nu decreasing)          the MI slope
  and they are not independent. From y = mu(x) x, dln y / dln x = 1 + L_mu; from nu = x / y,
      dln nu / dln y = dln x / dln y - 1 = 1/(1 + L_mu) - 1   ==>   (1 + L_mu)(1 + L_nu) = 1  EXACTLY.
  So ONE number decides every deep-external-field comparison below. That is why the MG and MI answers come
  out as sqrt(1+L_mu) and 1/(1+L_mu): reciprocal-flavoured, and on OPPOSITE SIDES OF 1.

  sympy, generic mu(x):   L_mu = x*Derivative(m(x), x)/m(x),   L_nu = -x*Derivative(m(x), x)/(x*Derivative(m(x), x) + m(x))
  (1 + L_mu)(1 + L_nu) - 1 = 0
  [OK] K1 the identity (1+L_mu)(1+L_nu) = 1 holds for an ARBITRARY kernel mu(x), proved symbolically with sympy on an undetermined function (residual 0). Every MG-vs-MI number below is therefore a statement about the single slope L_mu, and no kernel can decouple them
  [OK] K2 and it holds in the ACTUAL Route A numerics over eleven decades to 1.4e-10, with L_nu taken from a finite difference of nu() and L_mu from the module's analytic dmu_dx -- two independent routes through the kernel module, so a bug in either would break this

